  "query_preview": "..On the large scale, an upper low was centered along \nthe Nevada/Oregon state line, with a trough axis extending southward \nthrough California and high pressure aloft was centered over far \neastern Texas. The upper low will move slowly east into western \nWyoming by late Saturday, with the trough axis sliding across the \nGreat Basin and northern Arizona. A secondary low moves into the \ncentral California coast late Sunday into Monday. This secondary low \nwill then lift northeast through the intermountain west early to mid \nnext week, as high pressure aloft shifts westward into far southern \nTexas. \n\nSatellite layer blended total precipitable water imagery indicates \nPWATs were currently in the 1.0\"-1.2\" range across southeast \nArizona, with lower values of 0.50\"-0.75\" to our west across \nsouthern California and northern Baja. This drier air will begin to \nmove into the region from the west today as the upper low and trough \naxis shift eastward. There will still be enough moisture for a \nslight chance of showers and thunderstorms along the New Mexico \nstate line and in the White Mountains this afternoon/evening. \nModeled PWATs indicate values lowering to near 0.75\" by early this \nevening. \n\nSaturday looks to be the 'driest' day with model PW values around \n0.50\" across the forecast area and Arizona on the back side of the \naforementioned trough axis. By Sunday, model PW values start to \nincrease back to near 0.75\" as the secondary upper low moves into \nthe California coast and deeper moisture starts working back \nnorthward through eastern Sonora. While the forecast across \nsoutheast Arizona remains dry on Sunday, we will have to monitor any \nconvective outflow boundaries over Sonora which may push convection \nnorthward along the International Border in Cochise County. \n\nBy early to mid next week the low lifts across the Intermountain \nWest and the upper high begins to shift westward back across Texas, \nand increasing amounts of moisture move back into southeast Arizona \nfrom the east and south. The latest POP grids show a slight chance \nof showers and thunderstorms across Cochise County on Monday, with a \nslight chance/chance of showers and thunderstorms mainly east of \nTucson on Tuesday and then a chance of showers and thunderstorms \nfrom Tucson eastward Wednesday through Friday of next week. Modeled \nPW values by mid to late next week are back up into the 1.0\"-1.25\" \nrange across southeast Arizona.\n\nOtherwise, high temperatures through next Friday are within a degree \nor two of normal, with highs in the mid 90s in the Tucson metro, to \nnear 100 degrees across the western deserts and in the mid 80s to \nlower 90s across the valley locations of Santa Cruz, Cochise, Graham \nand southern Greenlee counties.",
